#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125404} :activity/id #uuid "92e4f0f3-75fd-5dde-9078-a3a161f5bd6a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125784 :tutorial/content "A linear transformation ![](images/93/9389ee0220ab92f8f743e705c7f9bef43f0f5c1e6122790e619ae64d99e4a480.svg) is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) such that \n\n![](images/e4/e43ee86a504bad240bf68cc1110136b16fbb9ddb7bb250e4146582d928685821.svg)\n\nfor every vector ![](images/80/807f62e69b36dbb97ce90919d746dc86cd890eaeab263a25d6287af18af8a598.svg) The transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) is called the **inverse** of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n*If ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is the standard matrix of ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg), then ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is invertible if and only if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is an ![](images/3e/3e214695b86d4170ea46f8cd0f8a4fd78e3d1c3a6abab1e8de1e3c35634c10a1.svg) matrix, then \n\n![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible ![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg)\n![](images/28/281f907860a395891d590680f527a1eee8be046879da8f3607669a22cae5e5d2.svg)\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a linear transformation with the standard matrix ![](images/70/70836ef8c789fb64a4b4a4b29a48074c146b8374116c1eeca4e3251def9e7d80.svg)\n![](images/96/9607abc75a2c67c4a11635cb9c84d6f575582d9961a822bdbf3d5e056536a9ce.svg)\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a bijection (invertible) ![](images/dc/dccddb0dd79fd52c99a28752fdcad781611226a29dc7f807411c349815f8442b.svg) \n![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg)  ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible" :tutorial/id #uuid "0d48d63e-a4e4-58a3-9520-cc4eaff29749" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3531 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6244a8ce-91c2-5c7a-8b6b-8f1b8c3887ce" :step/math-academy-id 20018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459245}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "260959de-4a00-5de6-b086-941592cccc26" :step/math-academy-id 20020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124435 :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459233}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "cd09bb01-66a3-55db-bfd3-d51ed79661a9" :step/math-academy-id 37610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124436 :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049903}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459301}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0cb9a218-ad79-5dd3-b698-91a9f29f4e9d" :step/math-academy-id 37611 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125405}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124747}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707} :activity/id #uuid "a321f5ff-d1ce-5778-90d0-3e0d34ad246d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10238c-7d05-58b9-96e8-9c69a8801161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417685} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eb1104-92b0-5dcf-bd12-07d9a0132bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417691} {:answer/id #uuid "d5329d0a-dafa-5056-a8ca-478564ecacb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417693} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaaa408-6e32-550a-b040-2adb608fe8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417696} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e2579-8b62-5595-a1e0-8ecfe4516933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417696} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b17b4e5-ae42-5c71-8493-ac975ab35b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c77aeb41-578d-533f-87d1-e33216f35c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84053" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0$ and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "291db6d3-4728-529e-a9be-f15ca20db25d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bdc2284-27e4-5704-8274-3bbae725fe40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417687} {:answer/id #uuid "86137dd0-1a5b-577a-bcd9-2df89f80ba0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417689} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba47e3f-5df2-5d3d-9f48-fd9b4dac64f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417690} {:answer/id #uuid "e41cc51f-809d-5496-8f87-309d33b65dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417694} {:answer/id #uuid "eb26f8f1-623e-5c62-85e0-a07163ca023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417689} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9bf687-3349-5509-9785-9f7978d92cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a059dc0b-5296-5e25-93e6-3ffd38125f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47556" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1,-1]^{T}\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$\n\nIII. $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3efa04eb-3c5c-549f-ac5a-28d6ca38cb8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a8fd7b-d587-5f84-abef-00aeb093eb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c74f36-a3a0-589c-a45b-af2f83dc7942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} :answer-field/id #uuid "88425be4-23e4-530d-894d-7ba2ee67cacc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b1c9af-14b9-5566-a390-e0cfcfb93713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453894} {:answer/id #uuid "56beb87c-fb76-5782-843d-1bb2ef34ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eccd02e-cd48-5da5-a4d4-40a643e437ff" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cc4c4f-6b01-5029-937b-d1096d770d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} {:answer/id #uuid "998a225c-9aad-55b1-9522-e1b176e1886c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b61e149-64e1-57db-98c8-d0a11bc647eb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079" :question/math-academy-id "q-38893" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "8823976c-e582-552f-a786-db586ba8c928" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417706}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684}]}
